From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: do not alias flow counters onto unrelated flows

airoha_ppe_foe_get_flow_stats_index() maps a flow hash onto a counter
slot with

	*index = hash >= ppe_num_stats_entries ? hash - PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES
					      : hash;

On an EN7581 there are two PPEs, so ppe_num_stats_entries is 8192
(PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES * num_ppe) while PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES itself is
4096.  The two sides of that expression therefore disagree about the
size of the block being indexed:

  hash in [0, 8192)      -> index [0, 8192)      usable
  hash in [8192, 12288)  -> index [4096, 8192)   usable, but aliased
  hash in [12288, 20480) -> index [8192, ...)    dropped by the caller
  hash in [20480, 32768) -> index [...]          dropped by the caller

so hash 4096 and hash 8192 share slot 4096, hash 4097 and hash 8193
share slot 4097, and so on for 4096 slot pairs.  A flow reading another
flow's counters is worse than a flow reporting no counters.  On top of
that, 20480 of the 32768 hashes come out with an index past the end of
the block and are discarded, so 62.5% of flows can never be counted even
though the NPU was given a slot for them.

An SRAM entry owns the counter slot named by its own hash: the NPU is
told the size of the counter block and where it lives, and counts by the
same hash the PPE used.  Inside the block the mapping is therefore the
identity, and a hash outside it must not be counted rather than folded
back onto somebody else's slot.  Both callers already treat a non-zero
return as "no counters for this flow".

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -672,8 +672,18 @@
 	if (ppe_num_stats_entries < 0)
 		return ppe_num_stats_entries;
 
-	*index = hash >= ppe_num_stats_entries ? hash - PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES
-					       : hash;
+	/* Every SRAM entry owns the counter slot named by its own hash, and
+	 * PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES worth of them exist per PPE, so inside the
+	 * block the mapping is the identity.  A hash past the block names a
+	 * DRAM entry or an entry in the second PPE, and neither was handed
+	 * to the NPU to count: the old "hash - PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES" moved
+	 * those onto slots that belong to unrelated SRAM entries, so one
+	 * flow reported another flow's counters.  Report no counters.
+	 */
+	if (hash >= ppe_num_stats_entries)
+		return -ENOENT;
+
+	*index = hash;
 
 	return 0;
 }
